ding volumes and creating long-term value for stakeholders.|Continuing to review its investment strategy with a view to balancing risk and return.","expansion":"","mdna":"Demand for quartz-based products continues to be driven by the construction, ceramics, foundry and glass industries, and, increasingly, by electronics and solar applications that require high-purity quartz.|The Quartz segment of the broader industry continued to see growing domestic demand, with rising adoption of quartz-based products in housing, commercial and interior applications.|Imposition of steep US safeguard tariffs (up to 50%) on engineered quartz surface products from India emerged as a significant headwind for the export-oriented, value-added segment of the Indian quartz industry; a slowdown there could have an indirect moderating effect on demand within the Rajasthan quartz-processing ecosystem.|The Company's investment portfolio provides a stable base of other income through interest and dividends but remains exposed to market-linked fair value fluctuations.|Management is confident of capitalizing on continued domestic construction and infrastructure activity and the growing use of high-purity quartz in electronics and solar applications, while remaining watchful of near-term headwinds affecting the broader industry.","risks":[{"risk":"Trading volume decline","note":"Revenue from operations fell sharply (Rs. 57.38 lakh vs Rs. 157.28 lakh in the previous year on the standalone MD&A figure), reflecting lower trading volumes; the Company is still working to rebuild volumes."},{"risk":"US safeguard tariffs on quartz exports","note":"Steep US safeguard tariffs (up to 50%) on engineered quartz surface products from India are a headwind for the export-oriented, value-added segment of the industry; a slowdown there could indirectly moderate demand within the Rajasthan quartz-processing ecosystem the Company operates in."},{"risk":"Investment portfolio market risk","note":"The Company's investment portfolio provides other income but is exposed to market-linked fair value fluctuations, evidenced by unrealised diminution recognised through Other Comprehensive Income during the year."},{"risk":"Industry/economic downturn and trade-policy risk","note":"Cautionary statement flags downturns in the industry globally and domestically, changes in India's political/economic environment, and evolving regulatory and international trade requirements including import tariff changes by overseas jurisdictions as factors that could affect performance."},{"risk":"Interest and exchange rate fluctuations","note":"Cautionary statement explicitly flags fluctuations in interest and exchange rates as a risk factor."},{"risk":"No dedicated Risk Management Committee","note":"The Company does not fall under the top 1000 companies for which a Risk Management Committee is mandatory under Regulation 21(5) of the SEBI Listing Regulations, and instead relies on its own internal risk-identification procedure rather than a board-level RMC."},{"risk":"Labour relations issues","note":"Cautionary statement in the MD&A explicitly lists labour relations issues among factors that could affect performance."}],"as_of":"2026-09-07","concallIntel":{}},"topics":{"one_liner":{"title":"One Liner (RHP)","body":"A Udaipur-based trading company dealing in Quartz Grit, Powder and related consumables along with other raw material trading, supported by an investment portfolio.","src":"annual_report","as_of":""},"business_model":{"title":"Business (RHP)","body":"The company's business primarily comprises trading in Quartz Grit, Powder and related consumables, along with other raw material trading activities, supported by an investment portfolio. Its business is centred on the domestic Quartz Grit, Powder and raw material trading segments rather than finished export-oriented quartz products.","src":"annual_report","as_of":""},"group_structure":{"title":"Related (RHP)","body":"- **Rupal Holdings Private Limited** Associate company since 08-03-2015; Company holds 49.00% shareholding; significant influence demonstrated by holding 20% or more of the total voting power.\n- **Emerald Construction Co. Pvt. Ltd** Counterparty for Project Advance transactions.\n- **Mumal Mining Company** Counterparty for business transactions.\n- **Ashok Drilling Company** Counterparty for business transactions.\n- **Mumal Enterprises** Counterparty for business transactions and Project Advance.\n- **Madhav Surface LLC** Counterparty for business transactions.","src":"annual_report","as_of":""},"end_markets":{"title":"End Markets (RHP)","body":"- construction\n- ceramics\n- foundry\n- glass\n- electronics\n- solar\n- domestic (India)","src":"annual_report","as_of":""},"demand_drivers":{"title":"Mdna (RHP)","body":"- Demand for quartz-based products continues to be driven by the construction, ceramics, foundry and glass industries, and, increasingly, by electronics and solar applications that require high-purity quartz.\n- The Quartz segment of the broader industry continued to see growing domestic demand, with rising adoption of quartz-based products in housing, commercial and interior applications.\n- Imposition of steep US safeguard tariffs (up to 50%) on engineered quartz surface products from India emerged as a significant headwind for the export-oriented, value-added segment of the Indian quartz industry; a slowdown there could have an indirect moderating effect on demand within the Rajasthan quartz-processing ecosystem.\n- The Company's investment portfolio provides a stable base of other income through interest and dividends but remains exposed to market-linked fair value fluctuations.\n- Management is confident of capitalizing on continued domestic construction and infrastructure activity and the growing use of high-purity quartz in electronics and solar applications, while remaining watchful of near-term headwinds affecting the broader industry.","src":"annual_report","as_of":""},"risks":{"title":"Risks (RHP)","body":"- Revenue from operations fell sharply (Rs. 57.38 lakh vs Rs. 157.28 lakh in the previous year on the standalone MD&A figure), reflecting lower trading volumes; the Company is still working to rebuild volumes.\n- Steep US safeguard tariffs (up to 50%) on engineered quartz surface products from India are a headwind for the export-oriented, value-added segment of the industry; a slowdown there could indirectly moderate demand within the Rajasthan quartz-processing ecosystem the Company operates in.\n- The Company's investment portfolio provides other income but is exposed to market-linked fair value fluctuations, evidenced by unrealised diminution recognised through Other Comprehensive Income during the year.\n- Cautionary statement flags downturns in the industry globally and domestically, changes in India's political/economic environment, and evolving regulatory and international trade requirements including import tariff changes by overseas jurisdictions as factors that could affect performance.\n- Cautionary statement explicitly flags fluctuations in interest and exchange rates as a risk factor.\n- The Company does not fall under the top 1000 companies for which a Risk Management Committee is mandatory under Regulation 21(5) of the SEBI Listing Regulations, and instead relies on its own internal risk-identification procedure rather than a board-level RMC.\n- Cautionary statement in the MD&A explicitly lists labour relations issues among factors that could affect performance.","src":"annual_report","as_of":""},"strategy":{"title":"Strategies (RHP)","body":"- Exploring new avenues of growth, including diversification of product offerings and entry into select markets, while remaining mindful of evolving global trade dynamics affecting the wider quartz industry.\n- Focus on rebuilding trading volumes and creating long-term value for stakeholders.\n- Continuing to review its investment strategy with a view to balancing risk and return